A||||10||ChadMan||Service life of shocks/struts?||||5||
Z||000000||ChadMan||05-18-2001||08:50 AM||||What's a reasonable service life to expect out of a set of shocks and struts?  I put a set of KYB's on my 88 Coupe 5 years and 54000 miles ago...how much more life can I expect out of them?<P>Thanks!||||reg||5
Z||000001||terryk||05-18-2001||05:51 PM||||Based on my calculations, 10 days, 15 minutes and 35 seconds.||||reg||1
Z||000002||PBJ||05-18-2001||09:07 PM||||monroe seams to think about 80 000km or<BR>60 000 miles would be a good service life.||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||05-18-2001||10:14 PM||||I wish there was a hard-fast rule, but there isn't. If it meets the bounce/jounce test and there is no fluid leaking out, odds are they are good.<P>Everytime I've replaced struts and shocks because they tested good, but "have to be bad, their so old!", they were really good and nothing changed.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Taijiguy||05-18-2001||10:33 PM||||Go with Koni's- lifetime  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Taijiguy||05-18-2001||10:33 PM||||Aww crap....a perfect record of no double posts just shot to hell.<p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 05-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||05-19-2001||11:36 AM||||IMHO Koni's are overkill and not street friendly. If you are interesting in any civility to the car, don't use Koni's. <P>Excellent shock, but they aren't valved for the street. Even at full soft, they are still too much. I had them on my X-11 and ridden in a few Fiero's with them and lord were they nice, but made the car generally unpleasant to cruise in. <P>Unless I'm racing and winning money, I'm not paying the Koni big bucks to make the car that unpleasent to drive.<P>The *best* isn't always the best.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 05-19-2001).]||||reg||13
Z||000007||Taijiguy||05-19-2001||12:58 PM||||Geeze Terry, whaddayawantfromlife? LOL<BR>I have them on all four corners, full stiff, and I wish I could get them <B>tighter</B>! I love the feel. Hey, this is a sports car by God. If you want a nice ride, get a caddy.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||05-19-2001||01:09 PM||||I agree W/ Terry... There's no hard fast rule for shock/strut life. Modern shock/strut product last much better then previous generations. It will usually perform "like new" untill a seal finally quits and leaks out the oil.<P>Shocks don't usually last as well as struts but don't be suprised to get 80-90,000 miles out of them. (assuming you don't blow one on a huge bump or abuse them like certain people...) Struts can last over 100,000 miles in most cases. <P>I have to agree with not putting Koni on daily drive cars. Koni designs pretty much everything for race use. I have to go over railroad tracks and other rought roads. Even with it's worn struts, My 87SC is stiff enough to rattle mu teeth doing a good bump. Just how stiff the end result is will depend on just what springs are on the car. Myself, I'm putting Monroe or KYB on it when I finally get that far... ||||reg||1
Z||000009||terryk||05-19-2001||04:34 PM||||Hey Taijiguy, don't ever disagree with me! Right or wrong, I'm right! Got that?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I love the way Koni's work when they are needed (cornering, etc.) but at some point I don't want to bend the chassis when I drive over an ant.<P>I have KYB's/Eibach's on my Ciera GT/SC and it's about right. Monroe's are nice too. The valving ratio's on those are more street and less Baja.||||reg||1
